  "series": "BASH QUOTING & EXPANSION — PART 2",
  "date": "07/09/2026",
  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "Forwarding Arguments",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "One Word Per Argument",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`\"$@\"` becomes `\"$1\" \"$2\" ...`.",
            "Each positional stays its own fully-quoted word.",
            "`\"$*\"` becomes `\"$1c$2c...\"`, one single word.",
            "`c` is the first character of `IFS`, a space by default."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Args a and 'b c'",
            "Result"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`\"$@\"`",
              "2 arguments"
            ],
            [
              "`\"$*\"`",
              "1 arg: `a b c`"
            ],
            [
              "`$@`",
              "3 arguments"
            ],
            [
              "`$*`",
              "3 arguments"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Unquoted `$@` and unquoted `$*` are identical.",
            "Both split and glob every argument.",
            "Both are wrong in a wrapper."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Zero-Argument Case"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "no args, \"$@\"",
                "lines": [
                  "expands to zero words",
                  "`cmd \"$@\"` runs `cmd`",
                  "safe"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "no args, \"$*\"",
                "lines": [
                  "expands to one word",
                  "the word is empty",
                  "`cmd` gets a phantom arg"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Wrappers"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "exec real-command \"$@\"   # boundaries exact",
            "args=(\"$@\")              # copy, still exact",
            "cmd $*                   # never do this"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`cmd $*` is the classic break on paths with spaces.",
            "Inside a function `$1` is the function's argument.",
            "But `$0` stays the script name."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "shift And set --"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do",
            "  case $1 in -v) v=1 ;; esac",
            "  shift            # shift n drops n",
            "done",
            "set -- a b c       # replace the positionals"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`shift` past the end returns non-zero, so the loop is safe.",
            "`set --` is the standard re-parse trick after `getopts`.",
            "`\"${@:2}\"` is everything from the second on.",
            "`\"${@:$#}\"` is the last argument."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "When is `\"$*\"` the right choice?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Joining arguments into one log message.",
            "Building a delimited string with a custom `IFS`.",
            "Never for forwarding to another command."
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "(IFS=,; echo \"$*\")   # prints a CSV row"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "The Bare for Loop",
          "items": [
            "`for x; do ...; done` has an implicit list.",
            "That list is `\"$@\"`, already quoted.",
            "Which is why the bare form is idiomatic."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Where Missing Quotes Bite",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Empty Variable, Deleted Root",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "rm -rf $dir/     # dir empty -> rm -rf /",
            "rm -rf \"$dir\"/   # still deletes / if empty",
            ": \"${dir:?dir is required}\"   # the real guard"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Test Commands"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`[ $x = y ]` with empty `$x`: 'unary operator expected'.",
            "`[ \"$x\" = y ]` is the correct form.",
            "Legacy `[ \"x$x\" = xy ]` exists only because of this.",
            "Prefer `[[ ... ]]`: it is syntax, no splitting or globbing."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "One Exception Inside [[ ]]",
          "text": "Quoting is still required on the RIGHT of `==` — unquoted there means glob matching, which is usually not what a comparison wants."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "echo Is Not An Output Tool"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`echo $var` collapses whitespace and expands globs.",
            "`echo` may eat `-n`, `-e`, or backslashes.",
            "Behaviour varies by shell and by options.",
            "`printf '%s\\n' \"$var\"` is the reliable primitive."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Filenames Are Hostile"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flowchart",
          "spec": {
            "title": "legal in a filename",
            "rows": [
              [
                "n0"
              ],
              [
                "b0",
                "b1"
              ],
              [
                "c0_0",
                "c0_1",
                "c0_2"
              ],
              [
                "c1_0",
                "c1_1"
              ]
            ],
            "nodes": {
              "n0": {
                "text": "legal in a filename",
                "shape": "diamond"
              },
              "b0": {
                "text": "allowed",
                "shape": "box"
              },
              "b1": {
                "text": "forbidden",
                "shape": "box"
              },
              "c0_0": {
                "text": "spaces",
                "shape": "box"
              },
              "c0_1": {
                "text": "newlines",
                "shape": "box"
              },
              "c0_2": {
                "text": "quotes, globs",
                "shape": "box"
              },
              "c1_0": {
                "text": "`/`",
                "shape": "box"
              },
              "c1_1": {
                "text": "NUL",
                "shape": "box"
              }
            },
            "edges": [
              {
                "from": "n0",
                "to": "b0"
              },
              {
                "from": "n0",
                "to": "b1"
              },
              {
                "from": "b0",
                "to": "c0_0"
              },
              {
                "from": "b0",
                "to": "c0_1"
              },
              {
                "from": "b0",
                "to": "c0_2"
              },
              {
                "from": "b1",
                "to": "c1_0"
              },
              {
                "from": "b1",
                "to": "c1_1"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A leading dash is parsed as an option.",
            "Use `rm -- \"$f\"` or `rm \"./$f\"`.",
            "NUL being forbidden is why `-print0` works.",
            "Pair it with `xargs -0` or `mapfile -d ''`."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Substitution Damage"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "x=$(cat f)                  # trailing newlines gone",
            "x=$(cat f; printf x); x=${x%x}   # keep them",
            "> $f     # ambiguous redirect on 0 or 2+ words"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Status Traps"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "ssh host \"${cmd@Q}\"   # remote side re-parses",
            "export VAR=$(cmd)     # masks cmd's exit status",
            "VAR=$(cmd) || exit    # split, then export"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "eval, Backticks, and The Seven Rules",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`$(...)` nests cleanly; backticks need escaping per level.",
            "Retire backticks in new code.",
            "`eval` re-parses its argument through the whole pipeline again.",
            "Every byte of user data becomes shell code."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`printf %q` is the other safe quoting route.",
            "`set -e` does not fire in an assignment's command substitution.",
            "Nor for any command whose status is tested.",
            "Numeric contexts are the one quote-free zone: `$(( ))` and subscripts."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Seven Rules",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "1 — Quote every expansion: `\"$var\"`, `\"$(cmd)\"`, `\"${arr[@]}\"`.",
            "2 — Exceptions are few and deliberate (see below).",
            "3 — Use arrays, never a string, for a list.",
            "4 — `\"$@\"` to forward; `\"$*\"` only to join on purpose."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "5 — `-print0` / `-0` / `-d ''` across process boundaries.",
            "6 — `printf '%s\\n'` instead of `echo` for foreign data.",
            "7 — `${p##*/}`, `${f%.*}` instead of forking `basename`/`sed`."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Four Exceptions"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Context",
            "Why safe"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`[[ ]]` `(( ))`",
              "shell syntax"
            ],
            [
              "`x=$y`",
              "assignment RHS"
            ],
            [
              "`arr=($s)`",
              "splitting wanted"
            ],
            [
              "glob pattern",
              "must stay a pattern"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Debugging, Preamble, and Tools",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Worth memorising as a unit.",
            "`set -e` still has the holes from the gotchas page.",
            "Treat it as a helper, not a safety net."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "printf '<%s>\\n' \"$@\"   # exact argument boundaries",
            "declare -p var         # type + full quoting"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`declare -p` shows string vs indexed vs associative.",
            "Fastest way to settle 'what is this variable really'."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Closing Summary",
          "text": "The shell expands, then splits, then globs, then strips quotes. Quotes turn off the splitting and globbing steps. Arrays preserve boundaries a single string cannot."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Preamble"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "#!/usr/bin/env bash",
            "set -euo pipefail",
            "IFS=$'\\n\\t'      # split on lines and tabs only"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Tools"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`shellcheck` catches most quoting bugs statically.",
            "SC2086 is the most common finding in the wild.",
            "It reads: 'Double quote to prevent globbing and word splitting'.",
            "`set -x` traces after expansion; Bash 4.4+ quotes the trace."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Portability"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "Bash only",
                "lines": [
                  "arrays, `mapfile`",
                  "`${var,,}`, `${var@Q}`",
                  "`[[ ]]`"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "POSIX sh",
                "lines": [
                  "`\"$@\"`",
                  "`case`",
                  "`set --`"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Leave With This",
          "text": "If a variable is not inside double quotes, you have promised the shell it contains no spaces, no globs, and no surprises — and filenames break that promise every day."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "caption": {
    "text": "Part 2 of BASH QUOTING & EXPANSION. Every Bash script that breaks on a filename with a space breaks for the same reason: the shell expands, then splits, then globs, then strips quotes — and an unquoted variable opts into all of it. 10 pages of field notes on quoting rules, the expansion pipeline, IFS, globbing, parameter expansion, arrays, and \"$@\" vs $*. Save it before the next `rm -rf $dir/`.",
